  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Frontal boundary provides shower/few storm \nchances today.\n\nA backdoor front drops south to the VA/NC border today with\nshowers/few storms prevalent mainly north of U.S. 460 with less\nchances further south into NC. This front will bring shift in\nthe winds to the northeast/east north of it should keep cloud\ncover around through most of the day, with low clouds in place\ninto this evening. This will create a dichotomy on highs with \n60s north of I-64 and possibly as far south as ROA/LYH to mid \n80s in the NC Piedmont.\n\nFront lifts north tonight as a warm front with showers fading\nexcept toward WV/Alleghany of VA.\n\nKey Message 2: Strong front late Friday to bring blustery winds\nand much cooler temperatures going into the weekend.\n\nWe quickly transition out of the zonal flow with the approach of a \nhigh amplitude upper trough and sharp attendant sfc cold front late \nFriday into Saturday. Not expecting much in the form of storms with \nagain limited instability with this system and the greater chance of \nshowers looks to be in the west mountains. The main impacts from \nthis front will be enhanced gradient winds kicking in for Friday \nnight into Saturday morning and then some of the coldest air of the \nseason ushering in. It will definitely feel like a rollercoaster of \nchange as highs Saturday look to be mainly in the low 50s for much \nof the mountains and then upper 50s to low 60s for the Piedmont. \nSunday morning is going to have frost/freeze concerns for much of \nthe area with progged lows mainly in the upper 20s to low 30s for \nthe mountains and mid to upper 30s for the Piedmont. This could \nagain be the case for Monday morning as well.",
